WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.797 --> 00:00:24.271
As animals, we owe a lot to the&nbsp;
single-celled organisms that came before us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.812 --> 00:00:29.520
These are the organisms that laid the&nbsp;
chemical groundwork for how we live,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.152 --> 00:00:36.369
from the DNA and proteins within them to the&nbsp;
molecules they released into the environment.&nbsp;

00:00:37.087 --> 00:00:45.101
And eventually, some of those early single-celled&nbsp;
organisms had an evolutionary epiphany that laid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.101 --> 00:00:51.960
the groundwork for multicellularity: if&nbsp;
they banded together into a colony and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:57.240
worked together—blending their lives and&nbsp;
identities until the lines between them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:04.599
began to blur—then maybe they could accomplish&nbsp;
more together than they could on their own.&nbsp;

00:01:06.154 --> 00:01:13.080
There’s something humbling about looking at&nbsp;
our hands or feet and imagining the mixture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.880
of cells within them, and realizing&nbsp;
the lessons that keep those cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:25.789
bound together physically and biologically are&nbsp;
rooted in a very ancient study in cooperation.&nbsp;

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:34.140
And colonies persist to this day.&nbsp;
Single-celled organisms like Nostoc link&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:40.320
themselves together into chain-like colonies&nbsp;
that allow individual cells to be assigned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.774
specific tasks like nitrogen-fixation&nbsp;
that benefit the colony overall.&nbsp;

00:01:46.652 --> 00:01:53.460
But colonies aren’t restricted to single-celled&nbsp;
organisms. Animals have gotten in on this behavior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:59.359
too, stacking their multicellular selves&nbsp;
together for the same reason the Nostoc does:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.874 --> 00:02:07.338
because they can, and because it’s good for them.
Two particularly notable examples of this are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.338 --> 00:02:12.143
colonies of mossy bryozoa found&nbsp;
in waters throughout the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.143 --> 00:02:18.592
as well as coral that live in the ocean - and&nbsp;
also, as we’ve seen before, in coral farms.&nbsp;

00:02:19.377 --> 00:02:25.525
But there’s one animal colony that’s&nbsp;
a little bit…unusual. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.525 --> 00:02:27.631
that animal is the rotifer.&nbsp;

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:38.280
To be fair to rotifers, this is not a typical&nbsp;
behavior for all of them. There are more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:45.060
2,000 species of rotifers that we know of,&nbsp;
and only around 25 of them are known to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:50.700
glue themselves together into a colony.
You can see this arrangement a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:57.480
more clearly in this Sinantherina colony. The&nbsp;
rotifers are all glued to some kind of plant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:04.620
bound together through the cement-like substance&nbsp;
they secrete from their foot. They radiate outward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:11.880
from there, like petals on a flower, but like&nbsp;
petals that have a crown of cilia at their end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:18.463
to stir up water and draw in food.
Some of these colonies are large,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.463 --> 00:03:24.965
holding around 200 rotifers in one&nbsp;
group. Others are smaller, containing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.965 --> 00:03:33.000
only five or so individuals connected together.
And what makes rotifer colonies so different from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:41.299
say, Bryozoa or coral colonies comes down to&nbsp;
differences in how those colonies are made.&nbsp;

00:03:41.299 --> 00:03:48.240
Let’s take Bryozoa, for example. The process&nbsp;
starts with an individual Bryozoa larva that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:54.000
attaches itself to some kind of surface,&nbsp;
like a rock. And once it has latched on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.360
it’ll split into two in a process called&nbsp;
budding, creating a clone that can divide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:07.980
again and again to produce a whole colony.
We have a whole episode on Bryozoa that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:13.150
can watch if you want to know more about&nbsp;
how these strange-looking organisms live,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:19.893
but the main thing you need to know right now is&nbsp;
that as these clones accumulate into a colony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.893 --> 00:04:25.140
their bodies are still connected in a way&nbsp;
that allows them to channel nutrients to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:32.791
each other. And coral colonies are similar,&nbsp;
surviving as a connected collective of clones.&nbsp;

00:04:34.749 --> 00:04:41.040
But rotifer colonies form in a few different&nbsp;
ways depending on the species, and none of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:47.400
bear much resemblance to the asexual budding&nbsp;
that drives coral and Bryozoa colony growth.&nbsp;

00:04:47.833 --> 00:04:54.079
In some species, colonies are formed through&nbsp;
what’s called autorecruitive colony formation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.079 --> 00:04:59.460
where a colony breaks apart, and creates&nbsp;
multiple factions that then grow in size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:07.260
as new rotifers are born and added to the colony.
Then there is what seems to us like the strangest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:14.769
method: some species use allorecruitive colony&nbsp;
formation, where young rotifers swim over to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.769 --> 00:05:21.240
sedentary adult rotifer and stick themselves&nbsp;
to the adult. The result can involve 50 or so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:29.100
young rotifers using a grown-up as a convenient&nbsp;
branch to live on. And I imagine it must be odd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:36.360
to go from life on your own to suddenly having&nbsp;
a rotifer daycare attached to you, but maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:42.060
it all makes more sense when you’re a rotifer.
And in the last route, called geminative colony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:49.680
formation, a bunch of baby rotifers swim to a&nbsp;
spot and gather into a colony. It sounds simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:57.000
enough, but it’s also my favorite one because&nbsp;
for some species, this process is kind of a mess.&nbsp;

00:06:00.089 --> 00:06:07.320
In 1906, a scientist named Frank M. Surface&nbsp;
documented how one species creates a new colony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:14.640
which begins when all the adults of a previous&nbsp;
colony lay eggs. It’s a coordinated and carefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:20.820
timed procedure that results a few days&nbsp;
later in a brood of baby rotifers connected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:28.020
to their parent colony by adhesive threads .
But then the mess begins. As the babies move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:35.280
those threads tangle together, creating a little&nbsp;
ball of baby rotifers that are still wriggling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:42.240
and jerking around until the ball breaks away&nbsp;
from its old colony. From there, the babies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:48.666
on their own, swimming as a ball until they find&nbsp;
a place to settle down into a permanent colony.&nbsp;

00:06:49.324 --> 00:06:55.860
However these colonies are formed, the&nbsp;
important—and unusual—thing is that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:01.440
don’t form in a way that allows the rotifers&nbsp;
to share with each other. They don’t seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:07.899
be exchanging nutrition or anything the way&nbsp;
that corals and Bryozoa seem to be doing.&nbsp;

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:14.262
So why? Why go through all the&nbsp;
trouble of forming a colony?&nbsp;

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:21.660
There are a few possibilities. While&nbsp;
the rotifers may not be sharing food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:26.820
gathering in these formations means that&nbsp;
they’re all generating currents around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:32.880
each other with their crown of cilia. So&nbsp;
perhaps—as scientists have considered—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:38.827
joint movement of these rotifers in this&nbsp;
formation helps them all gather more food.&nbsp;

00:07:38.827 --> 00:07:45.945
Or perhaps it makes them safer. A single&nbsp;
rotifer is an easy target for a predator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.945 --> 00:07:52.560
but a group—while conspicuous—is too big&nbsp;
to eat, making it an excellent hiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:58.920
spot for the individuals that live within it.
Or it could be that there is adhesive strength in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:06.960
numbers. As more rotifers attach themselves to a&nbsp;
surface, their glue reinforces each other, keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:13.408
them more securely locked in place and less&nbsp;
vulnerable to forces that might sweep them away.&nbsp;

00:08:13.887 --> 00:08:21.000
It could be one of these reasons, or multiple&nbsp;
of them, or perhaps something else altogether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:27.900
that we haven’t yet imagined. Whatever the&nbsp;
reasons, they belong to the rotifer, and to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:34.800
the microcosmos that tumbles around them and 50 of&nbsp;
their closest friends, glued together at the foot.&nbsp;

00:08:40.181 --> 00:08:45.910
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.&nbsp;

00:08:46.315 --> 00:08:48.900
